    "id": "guide-15",
    "slug": "electric-bike-kids-guide",
    "title": "Electric Bike Kids Guide: Ages, Speed Limits and Safety",
    "seoTitle": "Electric Bike Kids Guide: Ages, Speed and Safety",
    "excerpt": "Choosing an electric bike kids can ride safely: age and size, power and speed, helmets, state rules and when a balance bike or pedal bike is better.",
    "content": "An electric bike kids can ride gives young riders more range and confidence, and it lets the whole family travel further together. It also raises questions about age, power, speed and safety that are worth answering before you buy. This guide covers whether an e-bike suits your child, how age and wheel size match, what power and speed limiters do, helmets and protective gear, the rules for young riders in Australia and when a balance bike or ordinary pedal bike is the better choice. It ends with a short comparison of the kids and youth e-bikes in our range.\n\n![electric bike kids: Pedal Warrior 2 24'' Electric Mountain Bike Red](/images/products/pedal-warrior-2-24-electric-mountain-bike-red.webp#800x600)\n\n## Is an e-bike right for your child?\n\nAn e-bike suits a child who already rides well, understands road rules and can handle the bike's weight. The motor does not replace skill: it adds speed and mass, so a child needs good balance, braking and awareness before you add assistance. A child who still wobbles on a standard bike is better off practising first.\n\nIf your child is a confident rider and you live in a hilly area or ride long distances as a family, an electric kids bike can make outings far more enjoyable. It evens out the differences between an adult and a young rider and keeps the group together.\n\nThink about where your child will ride. Quiet streets and paths suit a first e-bike, while busy roads need more skill and supervision. Match the bike to the ride, not just to the child.\n\n## Age and size: 20, 24 and 26 inch\n\nKids' bikes are sized by wheel diameter, not by age. A 20-inch wheel typically suits children of roughly 6 to 9 years, a 24-inch wheel suits about 8 to 12 years, and a 26-inch wheel begins to suit teens and smaller adults. Height and inside leg length are better guides than age, so measure your child before you buy.\n\nBrowse our [electric bike for 10 year olds](/ebikes/kids-electric-bikes) range to see what is available, and check that your child can put both feet flat on the ground or at least touch the ground with the balls of their feet. A bike that is too big is hard to control and unsafe, even with a motor.\n\nMany parents search for an electric bike for kids by age, but frame size and confidence matter far more. Kids ebikes come in a narrow range of sizes, so measure and compare before you order.\n\n## Speed limiters and power\n\nThe motor on a children's e-bike should be modest, and the assistance should be smooth and predictable. Look for a bike with a clear speed limit and adjustable assistance levels that you can set to a low mode. In Australia, pedal-assist is limited to 250 W and 25 km/h for adult e-bikes under the EN 15194 standard.\n\nA lower speed limit is often more appropriate for a child. If the bike allows it, set the assistance to low and increase it as skills improve. Check that the brakes are strong enough for the bike's weight, and prefer hydraulic discs. Our [kids mountain bikes](/ebikes/electric-mountain-bike/kids-mountain-bikes) range includes models designed for younger riders.\n\nBattery size matters less for children than for adults, because most rides are short. A smaller battery keeps weight down, which makes the bike easier for a child to handle.\n\n## Helmets and protective gear\n\nA helmet is compulsory for every bicycle rider in Australia, and a child's helmet must fit properly. Look for the AS/NZS 2063 label, which [Standards Australia: AS/NZS 2063:2020 helmet standard](https://www.standards.org.au/blog/spotlight-on-as-2063-2020) explains. A youth bike helmet should sit level on the head, with the straps forming a V under each ear and room for two fingers between chin and strap.\n\nChoose a childs cycling helmet in a size that suits head circumference, not age. Our [childs cycling helmet](/kids-bike-helmets) range offers a spread of sizes and designs, including options with MIPS. Gloves, closed shoes and knee pads add comfort and protection, and a youth cycle helmet with a visor helps in bright sun and light rain.\n\nDo not forget gloves and eyewear. A fall on a bike with a motor is faster than on a standard bike, and good gloves protect the hands, which are the first thing to hit the ground.\n\n## Rules for young riders\n\nRules for young riders differ by state. Some states set an age at which children may ride on footpaths, and some have rules about riding in groups or on roads. Most states treat a pedal-assist bike that meets the standard as a bicycle, but check the rules for your state and local council.\n\n[ACCC Product Safety: e-bikes and e-micromobility devices](https://www.productsafety.gov.au/business/e-bikes-e-scooters-and-other-e-micromobility-devices) publishes safety guidance for e-bikes and other e-micromobility devices. Teach your child to give way to pedestrians, stop at crossings and ride predictably. Set clear routes and rules, and supervise young riders until you are confident they can manage on their own.\n\nInsurance may also be worth a look. Some home policies cover bikes within a value limit, and a child's e-bike can approach that limit, so check your cover before you buy.\n\n## Balance bikes vs e-bikes\n\nBalance bikes suit toddlers and preschoolers. They teach balance and steering without pedals, and they are light and safe. A standard pedal bike suits most primary-school children and builds fitness and skill. An e-bike is best kept for older children, usually from about 10 or 12, who already ride well and have a use for the extra assistance.\n\nIf your child is younger, consider a [trike bike australia](/electric-trikes) option such as a stable three-wheeler for confidence, or a standard bike for skills. Think about longevity too: kids grow fast, so a bike that fits for a couple of years is more sensible than one they will outgrow in a season.\n\nCheck the weight of the bike before you buy. A heavy e-bike is hard for a child to pick up after a fall, and a lighter frame makes the first few weeks far more enjoyable.\n\n## Kids e-bikes compared\n\nWe currently list a small number of kids and youth e-bikes. The [Pedal Warrior 2 24'' Electric Mountain Bike Red](/product/pedal-warrior-2-24-electric-mountain-bike-red) is a 24-inch electric mountain bike that suits older children and smaller teens. The Pedal Bandit 20-inch fat tyre bike is a compact option for younger riders who want a rugged bike. The table shows current prices.\n\nCheck the product page for the battery size, brakes and warranty, and add a helmet and a lock. Consider small upgrades such as comfortable grips and e bike pedals with good grip for wet conditions, which you can find in our [e bike pedals](/e-bike-accessories) range.\n\n| Model | Brand | Price (AUD) |\n| [Pedal Warrior 2 24'' Electric Mountain Bike Red](/product/pedal-warrior-2-24-electric-mountain-bike-red) | Pedal | $1,199 |\n| [Pedal Bandit 20\" Electric Fat Tyre Bike](/product/pedal-bandit-20-electric-fat-tyre-bike) | Pedal | $1,299 |\n\nPrices are the current listed prices at the time of writing and can change. Check each product page for the latest price and stock.\n\nAn electric bike kids will enjoy is one that fits them, keeps assistance low and comes with a helmet that fits. Take your time, test ride the bike with your child and make sure the whole family knows the rules. If you need help choosing, contact us with your child's height and riding experience.",
